   "number": "16",
   "title": "The machine image: seven eras",
   "kicker": "Video game eras",
   "lead": "Forty years of real-time graphics in seven frames. Every constraint here is a number: polygons, colours, texture memory.",
   "body": [
    "The NES gives a sprite three colours and a grid. The SNES gives it fifteen per palette row and parallax. PlayStation-era 2D keeps a painted sprite over a pre-rendered plate. PlayStation 3D drops to a few hundred warping polygons and makes texture memory the real budget. The arcade 3D of the late nineties smooths the mesh and adds a specular sheen. The PS3/PS4 era adds normal maps, ambient occlusion and cloth. The current generation ray-traces the light and grows hair strand by strand.",
    "Read this lesson as a history of what artists did with not enough, which is also the history of every other style in this course."
   ],
   "takeaway": "Technical limits are the most reliable style generator ever invented.",
